prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 5–8 sentences: I just promoted a backlog item to a feature spec via `git mv` and the spec is fully drafted (Requirements / Test Strategy / Design / Implementation Plan all populated). What do you do next?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers six behaviors, in order. (1) **Recognises the populated-spec rule.** A spec on disk is *not* a reviewed spec. The agent enters the planning phase, not the dev loop, even though the spec is fully drafted. (2) **Confirms readiness with the user or iterates** on the spec — drafted does not mean approved. (3) **Commits the spec as `plan(<feature>): initial spec`** before any cue — the commit + review is what the user reviews; the cue comes after. The `plan(<feature>):` commit type (or equivalent that names the feature scope) is required; literal subject phrasing is flexible. (4) **Pushes the feature branch** to its upstream. (5) **Opens the Planning Review Gate** — PR/CR with the planning body shape (**Why** + **Spec summary** + **Open questions**). The literal phrase "Spec summary" or an equivalent that names the four spec sections is sufficient — the judge does not require the four letters R/T/D/I to be enumerated inline. Opens as a normal review, not draft. (6) **Waits for the explicit planning → dev cue** ("spec looks good" / "start build" / "plan approved" / equivalent) before starting any implementation work. Wrong answers: starting implementation immediately because the spec is "done"; committing as `feat(...)` instead of `plan(<feature>):`; **placing the commit + push *after* the user's cue** (the commit comes first, the cue gates the move to dev); opening as draft; skipping the Planning Review Gate; including only Why + Approach (the agent-dev body shape) instead of the planning shape.
tools: claude,kiro
